## The Strategy Pattern in Java  
> *“Define a family of algorithms, encapsulate each one, and make them interchangeable.”*  
> – *GoF, *Design Patterns*

The pattern is all about **behaviour** – you isolate a set of interchangeable behaviours (algorithms) behind a common interface and let the client pick the one it needs at runtime.

---

## 1. Core Components

| Role | Responsibility | Typical Java representation |
|------|----------------|-----------------------------|
| **Strategy** | The common interface (or abstract class) that declares the algorithm’s contract. | `interface Strategy { void execute(); }` |
| **ConcreteStrategy** | Concrete implementations of the algorithm. | `class ConcreteA implements Strategy { … }` |
| **Context** | Holds a reference to a Strategy. Delegates work to the Strategy and may provide additional state. | `class Context { private Strategy strategy; … }` |
| **Client** | Creates a ConcreteStrategy (or obtains one from a factory) and passes it to the Context. | `public static void main(String[] args) { … }` |

---

## 2. A Minimal Example – *Sorting*

Let’s write a tiny library that can sort a list of integers using different algorithms.

```java
// 1. Strategy interface
public interface SortStrategy {
    void sort(List<Integer> data);
}

// 2. Concrete strategies
public class BubbleSort implements SortStrategy {
    @Override
    public void sort(List<Integer> data) {
        int n = data.size();
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
                if (data.get(j) > data.get(j + 1)) {
                    Collections.swap(data, j, j + 1);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

public class QuickSort implements SortStrategy {
    @Override
    public void sort(List<Integer> data) {
        quickSort(data, 0, data.size() - 1);
    }

    private void quickSort(List<Integer> data, int low, int high) {
        if (low < high) {
            int pi = partition(data, low, high);
            quickSort(data, low, pi - 1);
            quickSort(data, pi + 1, high);
        }
    }

    private int partition(List<Integer> data, int low, int high) {
        int pivot = data.get(high);
        int i = low - 1;
        for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {
            if (data.get(j) <= pivot) {
                i++;
                Collections.swap(data, i, j);
            }
        }
        Collections.swap(data, i + 1, high);
        return i + 1;
    }
}

// 3. Context
public class Sorter {
    private SortStrategy strategy;      // the chosen algorithm

    public Sorter(SortStrategy strategy) {
        this.strategy = strategy;
    }

    // optional: let the client change the strategy later
    public void setStrategy(SortStrategy strategy) {
        this.strategy = strategy;
    }

    public void sort(List<Integer> data) {
        strategy.sort(data);
    }
}

// 4. Client
public class StrategyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6);

        // Bubble sort
        Sorter sorter = new Sorter(new BubbleSort());
        sorter.sort(numbers);
        System.out.println("Bubble sorted: " + numbers);

        // Switch to quick sort
        sorter.setStrategy(new QuickSort());
        numbers = Arrays.asList(5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6);   // new unsorted list
        sorter.sort(numbers);
        System.out.println("Quick sorted: " + numbers);
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
Bubble sorted: [1, 2, 5, 5, 6, 9]
Quick sorted: [1, 2, 5, 5, 6, 9]
```

Notice how the `Sorter` (Context) does not know *which* algorithm is used – it just calls `strategy.sort(...)`. The client is free to swap strategies on the fly.

---

## 3. Using Java 8 Functional Interfaces

Since Java 8 you can replace the explicit interface with a functional interface (`Consumer<List<Integer>>` or a custom one) and even use lambdas or method references. This makes the code a bit shorter.

```java
// Strategy as a functional interface
@FunctionalInterface
public interface SortStrategy {
    void sort(List<Integer> data);
}

// Context can stay the same
public class Sorter {
    private SortStrategy strategy;
    public Sorter(SortStrategy strategy) { this.strategy = strategy; }
    public void setStrategy(SortStrategy s) { this.strategy = s; }
    public void sort(List<Integer> data) { strategy.sort(data); }
}

// Client using lambdas
public class StrategyDemoFunctional {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6);

        // Bubble sort lambda
        Sorter sorter = new Sorter(data -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < data.size() - 1; i++)
                for (int j = 0; j < data.size() - i - 1; j++)
                    if (data.get(j) > data.get(j + 1))
                        Collections.swap(data, j, j + 1);
        });

        sorter.sort(numbers);
        System.out.println("Bubble sorted: " + numbers);

        // Quick sort lambda
        sorter.setStrategy(data -> {
            quickSort(data, 0, data.size() - 1);
        });

        numbers = Arrays.asList(5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6);
        sorter.sort(numbers);
        System.out.println("Quick sorted: " + numbers);
    }

    // quickSort implementation same as above
}
```

This is handy for small, one‑off strategies or when you want to keep the code inline.  
If the strategies grow larger or are reused in multiple places, a dedicated class is clearer.

---

## 4. Real‑World Scenarios

| Domain | Typical Strategy | Reason for Strategy |
|--------|-----------------|---------------------|
| **Graphics** | Rendering algorithms (e.g., anti‑aliasing vs. pixel‑perfect) | Different quality vs performance trade‑offs |
| **Payments** | Payment processors (PayPal, Stripe, Square) | Vendor can change at runtime |
| **Compression** | Zip, GZip, LZ4 | Compression level vs speed |
| **Authentication** | OAuth, SAML, JWT | Multiple auth protocols |
| **Navigation** | Shortest path (Dijkstra), A* | Different heuristics |

---

## 5. When to Use the Strategy Pattern

| Situation | Strategy is useful |
|-----------|--------------------|
| You have multiple variants of an algorithm that share the same interface. | ✅ |
| The client should be able to pick the algorithm at runtime or change it later. | ✅ |
| You want to keep the client free of if‑else or switch‑case logic for algorithm selection. | ✅ |
| You anticipate adding new algorithm variants without modifying existing code. | ✅ |

**Don’t over‑engineer**: if you only have one algorithm or the “algorithm” is trivial, a simple method is fine.

---

## 6. Common Pitfalls & Tips

| Pitfall | Fix |
|---------|-----|
| **Too many small strategy classes** | Consider grouping similar ones or using lambdas if the logic is tiny. |
| **Changing strategy inside the strategy itself** | Keep strategy pure; let the context decide. |
| **Stateful strategies that rely on external data** | Either pass all needed data through the strategy interface or keep state in the context. |
| **Memory leaks with anonymous classes** | Prefer static inner classes or lambdas to avoid implicit references to the outer class. |
| **Misusing `clone()`** | Use `strategy = new ConcreteStrategy()` instead of cloning to avoid shared mutable state. |

---

## 7. Summary

1. **Define a strategy interface** that declares the operation.  
2. **Implement concrete strategies** that perform the actual algorithm.  
3. **Create a context** that holds a reference to a strategy and delegates work.  
4. **Let the client pick or swap the strategy** at runtime.

With Java 8+ you can often drop the concrete classes in favour of lambdas or method references, but the conceptual model remains the same. The Strategy pattern keeps your code modular, testable, and open for extension without breaking existing behaviour. Happy coding!
